## DESCRIPTION
## Solve a maximimum linear optimization problem that is not in standard form.
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## KEYWORDS('lines')
## Tagged by jjh2b

## DBsubject('WeBWorK')
## DBchapter('Linears and Linear Function')
## DBsection('Problem')
## Date('September 5, 2023')
## Author('Peter Staab')
## Institution('Fitchburg State University')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',             'PGML.pl',
    'parserRadioMultiAnswer.pl', 'tableau.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context('Point')->variables->are(x1 => 'Real', x2 => 'Real', x3 => 'Real');
Context()->noreduce('(-x)-y', '(-x)+y');

# make sure the level sets of the objective function is not parallel to
# the line.
do {
    ($a1, $b1) = (random(1, 4), random(1, 4));
    ($a,  $b)  = (random(2, 5), random(2, 5));
} while ($b * $a1 == $b1 * $a);

$d1 = random(4, 6) * $a1 * $b1;
$d2 = random(1, 3);

$line1 = Formula("$a1 x1 +$b1 x2")->reduce;
$obj   = Formula("$a x1 + $b x2")->reduce;

$line2 = Formula("$a1 x1 + $b1 (x3+$d2)")->reduce;
$line3 = Formula("$a1 x1 + $b1 x3")->reduce;
$d3    = $d1 - $b1 * $d2;
$obj2  = Formula("$a x1 + $b ($d2-x3)")->reduce;
$obj3  = Formula("$a x1 - $b x3 + $b*$d2")->reduce;

$tab = Tableau->new(
    A => Matrix([ [ $a1, $b1 ] ]),
    S => Matrix([ [1] ]),
    b => Vector($d3),
    c => Vector($a, $b),
    z => $b * $d2,
);

$orig_tab = $tab->copy;

($sol_tab, $flag) = phase2_solve($tab, 'max');
$state = $sol_tab->statevars;

$max = Real($state->[3]);
$x2  = $d2 + $state->[1];
$pt  = Point($state->[0], $x2);

$rma = RadioMultiAnswer(
    [
        ['There is no maximum.'],
        [ 'The maximum is %s and occurs at the point %s', $max, $pt ]

    ],
    1
);

BEGIN_PGML

Solve the following linear programming problem using the simplex method.
Note that this problem is not in  *standard* form.

Maximize [`f(x_1,x_2) =  [$obj] `] subject to

[```
\begin{cases}
[$line1] \leq [$d1],& \\
x_2 \geq [$d2],& \\
x_1 \geq 0.&
\end{cases}
```]

[_]{$rma}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
First, we need to rewrite this into one in standard max form. The objective is correct as is the first inequality.  The second inequality is a constrained problem.  If we let [`x_3 = x_2-[$d2]`], then the second inequality turns into [`x_3 \geq 0`].  Plugging [`x_2 = x_3+[$d2]`] into the objective and the first inequality or
[``` [$line2] \leq [$d1] ```] or
[``` [$line3] \leq [$d3] ```]

The objective can be written [`z = [$obj2] = [$obj3] `]

The tableau can then be written
>> [@ lop_display($orig_tab, toplevel => ['\(x_1\)', '\(x_3\)', '\(x_4\)', 'z', 'b']) @]* <<

Performing a single pivot, we get:

>> [@ lop_display($sol_tab, toplevel => ['\(x_1\)', '\(x_3\)', '\(x_4\)', 'z', 'b']) @]* <<

This shows that [`x_1 = [$state->[0]], x_3=[$state->[1]]`] and [`z=[$state->[3]]`].
Lastly, we translate [`x_3`] back to [`x_2`], so [`x_2 = [$x2]`]

Note: since the second inequality is [`x_2 \geq [$d2]`] then naturally it has a nonnegative inequality.  Thus, this can be solved with introducing a new variable.

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
